<!--
# Mail, relayed through Amazon SES

`mail.bsx` minus nothing and plus one spread. The stack is the same box, the
same database, the same buckets and the same domain; the only difference is
`{SesRelay}` beside the `<MailDomainBlock/>`, and everything below follows from
it.

## What the spread adds

A sending identity per domain, and a configuration set beside it that collects
reputation metrics and suppresses addresses that bounce or complain, scoped to
this domain so a newsletter cannot suppress anyone's mail but its own. Three
Easy DKIM `CNAME`s whose selector tokens are computed by the apply. A custom
MAIL FROM subdomain (`bounce.example.com`) with its own `MX` and SPF, which is
what makes SPF *aligned* with the header From and so a second way to pass DMARC.
An apex SPF of `v=spf1 include:amazonses.com -all` rather than the direct
`v=spf1 mx -all`.

It also adds the one static credential this stack has: SMTP AUTH is a fixed
username and password by protocol, so the box's render emits an IAM user whose
only permission is `ses:SendRawEmail`, an access key, and two SecureString
parameters holding the key id and the SMTP password terraform derives from it.
The values transit tofu state, which is why a stack with a relay wants state
encryption. `<StalwartProvision/>` reads the pair back out and writes it into the
one `ses` relay route every SES-relayed domain sends on: one route, because one
IAM user sends for every identity in the account.

Note that the IAM user exists exactly while some domain relays through SES. Move
the last one off and the next apply removes it, which is the point of the relay
being composed rather than assumed.

## events_topic and alarms_topic are two different things

`alarms_topic` on the domain is where a reputation alarm fires, and every relay
has one. `events_topic` on the `SesRelay` is where the raw stream of bounce,
complaint, rejection and delay OBJECTS is published, and only SES emits that. In
practice they are the same topic, as here, and they are named separately because
one of them survives a change of relay.

Both are named topics rather than declared ones, because one topic serves every
domain in the account. Create it, and grant `SNS:Publish` to the
`ses.amazonaws.com` service principal under an `AWS:SourceAccount` condition:
without that grant SES fails to publish SILENTLY, and you discover it the first
time you go looking for a bounce that should have been recorded.

## Usage

    beet --main=examples/infra/mail_ses.bsx validate
    beet --main=examples/infra/mail_ses.bsx deploy

`validate` needs only `tofu`. Live deploy needs the same things `mail.bsx` does,
plus SES production access if you want to send to anyone who has not verified
themselves as a recipient. The probe works inside the sandbox either way: the
simulator address it sends to is always permitted.
-->
<CliServer {CallOnReady}>
<Router {(RequestLogger, HelpHandler)}>
	<CrateCheck features={["infra", "extra"]}/>
	<Stack app_name="mail-ses-example" region="us-west-2">
		<DeployRoutes/>

		<VpcBlock bx:ref="net" label="net"/>
		<RdsPostgresBlock bx:ref="db" label="db" database="mail" {VpcRef($net)}/>
		<S3BucketBlock label="mail-blobs" deploy_versioned=false force_destroy=false
			runtime_write=true object_versioning=true/>

		<StalwartBlock label="mail" hostname="mail.example.com"
			blob_bucket="mail-blobs"
			{(VpcRef($net), DatabaseRef($db))}
			dns={Cloudflare{authority:"mail.example.com", zone_id:"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef", proxied:false}}
			ssh_public_key="ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIexampleexampleexampleexampleexampleexam you@example.com"/>

		<!-- the whole delta. `{SesRelay}` on the domain relays this one; put it
		     on the `<Stack>` above and every domain under it inherits, which is
		     what a stack that relays everything through SES wants. -->
		<MailDomainBlock
			domain="example.com"
			mail_host="mail.example.com"
			dns={Cloudflare{authority:"example.com", zone_id:"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef", proxied:false}}
			alarms_topic="mail-example-alarms"
			{SesRelay{events_topic:"mail-example-alarms"}}
			mailboxes={[
				{localpart:"hello", admin:true},
				{localpart:"news"},
				{localpart:"probe"},
			]}
			aliases={[
				{localpart:"postmaster", target:"hello"},
				{localpart:"abuse", target:"hello"},
			]}/>

		<Route path="deploy" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<EnsureSecret secret="db-password" variable="db_password"/>
			<EnsureSecret secret="mail-admin-password"/>
			<EnsureDkimKey/>
			<TofuApply/>
			<EipReverseDns/>
			<StalwartProvision/>
			<MailProbe mailbox="probe" sender_domain="example.com"/>
		</Route>
	</Stack>
</Router>
</CliServer>
